}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{5}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}|=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{30}}.\n$$"] [17592186360505 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$K$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to matrix addition and scalar multiplication) because **the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix does not belong to $K.$***   \n\nIndeed, adding the entries of the $2\\times 1$  zero matrix yields $0+0=0\\ne 1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- addition of matrices is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- addition and scalar multiplication of matrices  satisfy the distributive laws. $✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot A=A$ for all $A\\in K.$ $\\,✓$"] [17592186360506 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$J$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of complex numbers and scalar multiplication of a complex number by a real number) because **multiplication of $z\\in J$ by real scalars is not closed.*** \n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =0$ and $z=i,$ then $\\alpha \\cdot z=0\\cdot i=0\\notin J.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition in $J$ is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition  in $J$ is commutative.  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication in $J$ satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- If  $z\\in J$ then $z=ai$ for some $a\\in R.$ Hence, $-z=(-a)i\\in J$   since $-a\\in R.$ $\\,✓$"] [17592186360532 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }A1\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }A4\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364351 "To compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & ={r}_{1}\\cdot {s}_{1}+{r}_{2}\\cdot {s}_{2}+{r}_{3}\\cdot {s}_{3}+{r}_{4}\\cdot {s}_{4}+{r}_{5}\\cdot {s}_{5} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3+4+3+5-4 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366822 "Starting from the equation $r\\cdot s=1,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $z$ and then solve for $z$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & =1 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot 2+6\\cdot (z+1)+z\\cdot (-9)+4\\cdot z+1\\cdot 7 & =1 \\\\ -6+6z+6-9z+4z+7 & =1 \\\\ z & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $z=-6.$"] [17592186368723 "Starting from the equation $l\\cdot m=0,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $y$ and then solve for $y$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l\\cdot m & =0 \\\\ -1\\cdot (y+1)+2\\cdot (-2)+y\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -y-1-4+2y+6-3 & =0 \\\\ y-2 & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y=2.$"] [17592186370894 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 7 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{26}{5} & \\frac{38}{5} & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are tw